### Description
The Terraform program below creates an AWS AMI for the latest Amazon Linux 2 AMI. It uses the `aws_ami` data source to fetch the latest AMI ID based on specific filters and then creates an `aws_ami_copy` resource to copy this AMI into the desired region. The program includes a provider configuration for AWS and sets a default region.

### Terraform HCL Program

```hcl
provider "aws" {
  region = "us-west-2"  # Default region, can be changed as needed
}

data "aws_ami" "latest_amazon_linux_2" {
  most_recent = true
  owners      = ["amazon"]

  filter {
    name   = "name"
    values = ["amzn2-ami-hvm-*-x86_64-gp2"]
  }

  filter {
    name   = "virtualization-type"
    values = ["hvm"]
  }
}

resource "aws_ami_copy" "latest_amazon_linux_2_copy" {
  name              = "copied-latest-amazon-linux-2"
  source_ami_id     = data.aws_ami.latest_amazon_linux_2.id
  source_ami_region = "us-east-1"  # Example source region, can be changed as needed
  description       = "Copied AMI of the latest Amazon Linux 2"

  tags = {
    Name = "copied-latest-amazon-linux-2"
  }
}
```

### Explanation
1. **Provider Configuration**: The AWS provider is configured with a default region (`us-west-2`). You can change this to any other valid AWS region.
2. **Data Source `aws_ami`**: This data source fetches the latest Amazon Linux 2 AMI using filters for the AMI name and virtualization type.
3. **Resource `aws_ami_copy`**: This resource copies the fetched AMI from the source region (`us-east-1`) to the target region (configured in the provider). It sets a name and description for the copied AMI and adds tags for identification.

This configuration ensures that you have the latest Amazon Linux 2 AMI available in your specified region, ready for use in your AWS infrastructure.

