This Terraform program sets up a basic AWS network infrastructure consisting of a Virtual Private Cloud (VPC) with a custom DHCP options set. The VPC is configured with a CIDR block of `192.168.0.0/16`. The DHCP options include a domain name (`windomain.local`), a combination of custom and Google's DNS servers, and a NetBIOS name server (`192.168.56.102`). These DHCP options are then associated with the created VPC, customizing its DHCP settings.

### Terraform HCL Program

```hcl
provider "aws" {
  region = "us-west-2"  # Specify the desired AWS region
}

resource "aws_vpc" "my_vpc" {
  cidr_block           = "192.168.0.0/16"
  enable_dns_support   = true
  enable_dns_hostnames = true
}

resource "aws_vpc_dhcp_options" "my_dhcp_options" {
  domain_name          = "windomain.local"
  domain_name_servers  = ["8.8.8.8", "8.8.4.4", "192.168.56.102"]
  netbios_name_servers = ["192.168.56.102"]
}

resource "aws_vpc_dhcp_options_association" "my_dhcp_options_association" {
  vpc_id         = aws_vpc.my_vpc.id
  dhcp_options_id = aws_vpc_dhcp_options.my_dhcp_options.id
}
```

This Terraform configuration creates a VPC with the specified CIDR block and enables DNS support and hostnames. It also defines custom DHCP options with the specified domain name, DNS servers, and NetBIOS name server, and associates these options with the VPC.

